  "summary": "Warm-hued silk squares printed with buta paisley — the scarf that speaks in Azerbaijan.",
  "description": "Kelaghayi — four-cornered silk headscarves printed with wooden stamps and dyed with madder, saffron and indigo — are the living silk of Azerbaijan's silk towns, above all Basqal and Sheki. The buta (paisley) motif, stars and plants are arranged in golden-cornered compositions; how a woman ties it signals age, mood and family event, from mourning to wedding. Silk-farming, boiling, dyeing and stamping form an entire seasonal economy documented by travelers for centuries; UNESCO inscribed kelaghayi making in 2014, and Basqal's workshops still print each square by hand in four-color passes.",
  "culturalContext": "Kelaghayi is Azerbaijan's silk-language: how a woman ties it signals age, mood and event; Basqal and Sheki workshops (UNESCO 2014) sustain a full sericulture economy, and the scarf bridges Azeri, Iranian and Turkish headwrap traditions as the country's most feminine heritage emblem.",
